A||||21||watts||88 Cradle swap||||1||
Z||000000||watts||11-03-2001||11:35 PM||||I'm pretty sure there's a few people here other than myself that are in the midst of putting 88 cradles into early model cars (an '87 in my case).<P>I'd like to toss out some things I've found that you might not expect, and see who else has what...<P>I'll start off with brake lines.  On the early cars, the calipers are to the front of the rotor.  88's are to the back.  You'll need custom flex hoses (I'm going braided stainless) to reach the hardline mounting point.<P>Anyone else want to add more?<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid||||reg||1
Z||000001||honegod||12-16-2001||02:47 AM||||that's IT ???||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadero2dmax||12-16-2001||04:18 AM||||Yep, that's it<P>EXCEPT<P>You need to get an adapter for the top of the strut, which requires drilling one hole at each tower near the original strut (the adapter will locate the exact place to drill it).<P>You will need to get an adjustable perch strut, (some people call these "coil-overs), because only a 2 1/2 inch spring will clear the sidewalls of the tower with the new hole location.<P>I have heard from a couple people that claim to have installed this cradle that the bolt holes need elongated. But I have done a total of 4 of these cradle swaps, and have yet to find onr that didn't just bolt directly up.<P>With the braided lines, strut relocation, and "coil-overs" it is a done deal!!<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to:  <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821</A>  <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||12-16-2001||05:40 AM||||would you happen to know how it affects how much backspace you have? i've been hoping the switch would let me use a wider wheel. <P>also, i've read you can't do the 6000 type brake swap to get a 115mm (4.5") bolt pattern for the rear. will some other junkyard swap work? maybe a newer gm car since it's a newer model? or did the design get more fiero specific (no interchanging)?||||reg||1
Z||000004||cadero2dmax||12-16-2001||07:41 PM||||The entire swap uses the '88 rear suspension, so it will keep the original offset.<P>Can't help you with the bigger wheel issue, I use the stock wheel size. I use a chrome Chrysler 14" wheel for the street, and stock wheels with 14" R-1 tires on the Challenge car.<P>Plus I enjoy the less than $100 per tire cost, and the better handling/acceleration the 14" gives me. I am probably going to have to go to 15's soon, there are fewer and fewer 14" performance tires available these days. <P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to:  <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821</A>  <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||honegod||12-17-2001||04:02 AM||||on the flex lines, I am needing to replace all 4 ( front and rear )anyway. will I need to buy two sets to get enough long hoses ?<P>as in does the front need the long hose too ?<P>( the weather here is horrible, cold and rainy or I would just LOOK )<P>I have tried searching the archives for the fabled Big Post on the swap but I suxors at searching, and came up dry, any guidence ?||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadero2dmax||12-17-2001||08:24 PM||||I don't know of any websites that have this particular mod, but you could keep your eye on the Grassroots Challenge Fiero list, photos will be on there soon.<P>You only need to buy one set of hoses.<P>G<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||watts||12-17-2001||09:48 PM||||Yep as he (^^) says, you only need to buy one set - but they're custom!  Problem is that the calipers are on the front side on the early ones, and the backside on the 88's.  So they're about a foot too short!<P>You need to get the kit from Held as well to make your struts fit, and it'll come with the proper coil over conversion as well.<P>I'll see what I have for photo's here...<P>Ok...  Here's what a coil over with the adaptor looks like:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coil_over_assembled.jpg"> <P>Here's what I meant by 'front and rear' on the calipers:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/New_brakes.jpg"> <P>Make more sense?<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadero2dmax||12-17-2001||10:04 PM||||Have you given thought to putting the shorter lines on the front? I have on 4 different '88 cradle swaps. Both my current Fieros have the '88 cradle, in addition to the Q4 racer, and an 85 Q4 street machine I had a few years back. I installed the longer of the lines on the rear, and the shorter on the front on all of them.<P>The shorter line on the front will still give plenty of flex for cornering if you take caution when you install it. The Q4 racer was roadraced for 2 years, then autocrossed for 5 seasons by me - and the past season by Team Python partner, Greg Duncan. All with the original Earl's braided lines installed in 1991. Lotsa turns were logged on THAT set- and with no failures in all that time!<P>G<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||honegod||12-17-2001||11:11 PM||||thanks for the help    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I believe I understand the hose requirements now. excellent !<P>on the parking brake cables, if I may further trouble y'all, I understand that the 88 rear cable setup is in order, but how does it interface with the 84 front cable setup ?<P>as in, where is the transition ?<BR>I assume that the handle requires no change, how about the cable that runs from the handle to where it splits into the individual caliper cables, will the 84 cable suffice, or will an 88 need to be installed in its place ?<P>the reason for all this inquisitivness is that I have not the capability to execute this swap here at home, my work area is currently a freezing swamp, so I needs must avail myself of another , who has more felicitious accomodations.<BR>unfortunatly the chap whom I hope to honor with the task seems disinclined to attempt the feat, through sheer ignorant fearfulness of the procedure.<BR>my thought is to swot up on as many of the possible complications as are likely, thus to better allay  and calm his quavering spirit.<P>hence my seemingly interminable questioning of intricate detail and procedure.<P><p>[This message has been edited by honegod (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||watts||12-18-2001||11:51 PM||||OK - Put down the thesaurus, and back away...<P>But anyhow - the best bet is to just grab all the cables from the '88 donor car.  I haven't exactly laid them all out beside each other or anything, but they appear to be all different.  I just took everything up to, but excluding the brake lever itself.<P>Bear in mind that there's not much cabling here - it goes from the lever to the cradle (one cable), then off to each caliper.  Not like a 'normal' big car where you've got an equalizer bar, and all the other crap.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ITB 2M4||12-19-2001||12:30 AM||||Do you absolutely have to use coilovers for this swap?  I can't use coilovers in Improved Touring anymore.  I've got the Koni suspension kit from the Fiero Store, and it seems to handle really great anyways.  At my Daytona Driver's School, and my Sebring race, I didn't have any such 'turn-in' problems as described by the FieroRacingList guys.<BR>Cadero, you for sure gonna be at GRM's $2002 challenge?  It's not that far away from where I live, and my friend Mike Guido has won both years of the Challenge with his Cosworth Vega powered MG.  I'd really like to go up there for that.  Maybe I'll see you there.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||5
Z||000012||watts||12-19-2001||12:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ITB 2M4:<BR><B>Do you absolutely have to use coilovers for this swap?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've never found another way to do it.<P>Problem is, that the '88 rear struts 'tip in' more that the early ones.  If you were to just stuff the 88' assembly in and early car (with no springs), the tip of the strut would be roughly 2" more towards the engine.  Ummm...  did that make sense????<P>Anyhow, point is you obviously CAN'T do that for obvious reasons!  heh...  the stock spring is so wide (5-6"?) that there's no room in the strut tower.  The coil over is only about 2-3", so there's enough room to have it tilted over.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||honegod||12-19-2001||02:27 AM||||thesaurus, heh. I have just finished reading 6 books by P.G.Wodehouse, a feat which tends to alter ones speech patterns in a somewhat marked manner.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>earlier you mentioned that the brake line replacements must be CUSTOM, implying that a normal SS braided teflon set does not meet the need. if so, whence must the 'custom' set originate ?<P>with the coilover spring in place the lower perch would no longer be the initial contact point for the rear tyres, currently I am limited to 205/55/16s on my grand am rims.<BR>with the 88 setup + coilovers, what becomes the limiting factor ?<BR>215/50s would go nicely, but for the perch.<p>[This message has been edited by honegod (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||cadero2dmax||12-19-2001||06:25 AM||||Again, you CAN buy custom brake lines if you feel the need, but don't have to. Your choice!!<P>The adjustable perch struts (coilovers) are necessary, the 2 1/2" spring and smaller perches allow the newly located strut to fit in the early chassis strut tower. <P>But you are right, at the new angle the '88 conversion puts the strut, and the smaller perches/springs on the adjustable perch (coil-over)strut allow quite a bit more clearance.<P>Are you sure adjustable perch struts are not allowed? My GCR description of adjustable perch is exactly what we are talking about here, not the GCR description of coil-overs.<P>G <p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||honegod||12-19-2001||06:40 AM||||&gt;&gt;&gt; you CAN buy custom brake lines if you feel the need, but don't have to. Your choice!! &lt;&lt;&lt;<P>understood, I was in no way trying to imply that I mistrusted your experience.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I was just trying to see if watts had some SPECIAL hoses in mind.<P> as I said, I am trying to get as much data as I possibly can on things that CAN go wrong with this swap.<BR> so if only one person has had a problem I want to know about it, and what it took to solve it, since whenever I do something I always seem to run into problems that have never happened to anyone else, you know how that goes, I'm sure.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Will||12-19-2001||01:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by honegod:<BR><B>with the coilover spring in place the lower perch would no longer be the initial contact point for the rear tyres, currently I am limited to 205/55/16s on my grand am rims.<BR>with the 88 setup + coilovers, what becomes the limiting factor ?<BR>215/50s would go nicely, but for the perch.<P>[This message has been edited by honegod (edited 12-19-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think your problem is your wheels. I'm running 16x8 wheels from a Turbo Grand Prix, and have plenty of room for 255/50-16's on my '87 GT.<P>Those Grand Am wheels must have a metric $h!t ton of offset. What year Grand Am?||||reg||1
Z||000017||watts||12-19-2001||02:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by honegod:<BR><B>&gt;&gt;&gt; you CAN buy custom brake lines if you feel the need, but don't have to. Your choice!! &lt;&lt;&lt;<BR>I was just trying to see if watts had some SPECIAL hoses in mind.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not really special hose itself, just the length.  I was trying to say that you can't simply unhook the flex line from the one, and slap it onto the other.  That's all I meant by custom.<P>Does anyone else know if some place is offering these prefabbed now?  There's a place not far from here that will make them all for me, as long as I'm only using it on my (nudge nudge, wink wink) off road race car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||cadero2dmax||12-19-2001||06:57 PM||||Martin White has a webpage that discusses this swap:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~martinwhite/fiero/cradle.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~martinwhite/fiero/cradle.html</A>  <P>G||||reg||1
Z||000019||honegod||12-20-2001||04:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR>[BI'm running 16x8 wheels from a Turbo Grand Prix, and have plenty of room for 255/50-16's on my '87 GT.<P>Those Grand Am wheels must have a metric $h!t ton of offset. What year Grand Am?[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>a look of blank bafflement crosses my normally serene features, I understood that grand prix rims were of a non fiero bolt pattern and therebye unsuitable for our casual application.<BR>are you using a different hub/brake assembly from the standard 88 arrangement ?<P>the GA rims I use are from a mid 90s, 16X6"<BR>a bit fey for really manly tyres, but I believe I could profitably shove the aforementioned 215/50s on the rear in a spring perchless application.||||reg||1
Z||000020||honegod||12-22-2001||07:51 AM||||I went and talked to the man.<BR>he wants $3000 up front to even consider attempting the swap for me, not counting parts.<BR>with my budget being $2000, INCLUDING parts, I guess I am S.O.L.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>unless anyone knows of someone competent to do the rear end swap who is within a couple hundred miles of Terre Haute indiana. ?????||||reg||1
Z||000021||Will||12-22-2001||08:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by honegod:<BR><B> a look of blank bafflement crosses my normally serene features, I understood that grand prix rims were of a non fiero bolt pattern and therebye unsuitable for our casual application.<BR>are you using a different hub/brake assembly from the standard 88 arrangement ?<P>the GA rims I use are from a mid 90s, 16X6"<BR>a bit fey for really manly tyres, but I believe I could profitably shove the aforementioned 215/50s on the rear in a spring perchless application.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As it says, I have an '87 GT (with early style rear suspension). I'm using A-body hub carriers, bearings, and brakes on the rear. <P>[quote]<B><BR>unless anyone knows of someone competent to do the rear end swap who is within a couple hundred miles of Terre Haute indiana. ?????<BR></B>[quote]<P>Umm... YOU!  Remember: righty tighty, leftie loosie. The swap really isn't that hard.||||reg||1
